        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "af2250c5-4069-5b02-b5cd-baaab6615809",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Crepis, Isatis\n- People: E. B. Babcock\n- Concepts: Asteraceae, Brassicaceae, polyploid apomictic genera\n\nFacts:\n- Crepis is a genus of annual and perennial flowering plants in the family Asteraceae.\n- Crepis is commonly called hawksbeard or hawk's-beard in some parts of the world.\n- Crepis superficially resembles the dandelion, but it usually has branching scapes with multiple heads, although solitary heads can occur.\n- Isatis is a genus of about 30 species of flowering plants in the family Brassicaceae.\n- Isatis is native from the Mediterranean region east to central Asia.\n- E. B. Babcock is particularly known for field investigations and extensive experiments on the polyploid apomictic genus Crepis.\n- E. B. Babcock recognized 196 species within Crepis.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
